course: dictionary
lesson: 10441
title: Strong verbs: trudge
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A strong verb paints a picture on its own. Instead of "walked slowly", you can write trudged, one word that shows how someone moved. The Mole «trudged off to the nearest village, a long way off, for milk and eggs» and the word tells you he was tired and the walk was hard going.
explain: To trudge means to walk slowly and heavily, because you are tired or because the ground is hard to walk on, such as snow, mud or sand. Trudge can also be a noun: a long, tiring walk. Phrases like trudge home or trudge up the hill help you picture it.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If "The Mole walked slowly and heavily to the village" still makes sense, you have it right. When you write, look for a weak verb with an adverb and ask yourself: is there one strong verb for this?
fact: To trudge means to walk slowly and heavily.
fact: Trudge can also be a noun: a trudge is a long, tiring walk.
fact: The verb forms are trudge, trudges, trudged and trudging; «trudging» means walking heavily.
fact: Use trudge when someone is tired, or when the ground is snow, mud or sand: trudge home, trudge through the snow.
fact: A strong verb can do the work of a weak verb plus an adverb: "walked slowly" can become trudged, crept or dawdled, each with its own picture.
exercise: «The Mole trudged off to the nearest village for milk and eggs.» What does trudged mean here? | *Walked slowly and heavily | Jumped over the stream | Flew high over the hills | Ran quickly and happily
exercise: Ella says, «The walk home from school was a long trudge.» What is a trudge here? | A quick game of tag | A loud shout | A kind of cake | *A long, tiring walk
exercise: Which word is the best strong verb for walking slowly and heavily through deep mud? | Skipped | *Trudged | Danced | Whistled
test: «Trudging» up the steep hill, Sam felt tired. What does trudging mean? | Sitting down to rest at the top | Running up the hill to win a race | *Walking slowly and heavily | Sliding down the hill on a sledge
test: Which phrase best shows someone walking heavily through sand? | Skipped along the beach | Dashed across the sand | Floated over the waves | *Trudged through the sand
exam: Which sentence uses trudge correctly? | The sunshine trudged through the window | The bird trudged across the sky, singing happily | I trudged a cake for my friend's party | *After the long hike, we trudged back to the campsite, tired and muddy
